{"type":"message","id":"4238d997","parentId":"05e4734e","timestamp":"2026-03-08T22:04:39.664Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[Sun 2026-03-08 23:04 GMT+1] You are Mistral AI, an expert systems architect.\n\n**Context:**\n- OpenClaw evolution system currently generates proposals via templates\n- Has a Python neuro-memory-agent with 8 brain mechanisms (Bayesian surprise, consolidation, two-stage retrieval, etc.)\n- Currently disabled - performance concern (Python IPC slow)\n- Goal: Make evolution learn from past failures/successes\n- Batch queue exists (10 events, 2s flush interval)\n\n**Question:** What's the best architecture to wire neuro-memory to evolution?\n\nConsider:\n1. Event-mesh integration vs evolution-only integration\n2. When to compute expensive Bayesian surprise\n3. How to leverage all 8 mechanisms (consolidation, forgetting, interference)\n4. Performance vs intelligence trade-off\n\nProvide:\n- Recommended integration points\n- What to store (surprise scores? outcomes? embeddings?)\n- When to retrieve (before proposal generation? after failure?)\n- How to handle consolidation (background? on-demand?)\n\nBe specific about which mechanisms to use and when."}],"timestamp":1773007479663}}
{"type":"message","id":"76d0fba8","parentId":"4238d997","timestamp":"2026-03-08T22:06:03.958Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a complex systems architecture question about integrating a neuro-memory system with an evolution system. Let me think through this systematically.\n\nKey constraints and context:\n- OpenClaw evolution generates proposals via templates\n- Python neuro-memory-agent with 8 brain mechanisms\n- Performance is a concern (Python IPC is slow)\n- Currently disabled\n- Goal: Learn from past failures/successes\n- Batch queue exists (10 events, 2s flush interval)\n\nThe 8 brain mechanisms mentioned:\n1. Bayesian surprise (expensive)\n2. Consolidation\n3. Two-stage retrieval\n4. Forgetting\n5. Interference\n6. (others not specified, but likely include things like pattern recognition, memory encoding, etc.)\n\nLet me think about the architecture:\n\n**Integration Points:**\nThe key question is whether to integrate via event-mesh or evolution-only.\n\nEvent-mesh integration:\n- Pros: Decoupled, can be used by other systems, follows event-driven architecture\n- Cons: More complex, potential latency, need to handle async\n\nEvolution-only integration:\n- Pros: Simpler, direct control, lower latency\n- Cons: Tight coupling, can't leverage memory elsewhere\n\nI think a hybrid approach makes sense - use event-mesh for learning/storage but provide a synchronous API for retrieval during evolution.\n\n**When to compute Bayesian surprise:**\nThis is expensive, so:\n- NOT on every event\n- Batch computation during consolidation\n- On-demand for critical decisions\n- Pre-compute during idle time\n\n**What to store:**\n- Proposal metadata (type, context, confidence)\n- Outcome (success/failure, metrics)\n- Surprise scores (when computed)\n- Embeddings of proposals (for similarity)\n- Temporal patterns\n- Error signatures\n- Context features\n\n**When to retrieve:**\n- Before proposal generation (to avoid past failures)\n- After failure (to analyze patterns)\n- During consolidation (to strengthen learning)\n\n\n- Background consolidation with periodic flushes, leveraging the existing batch queue and idle-time processing\n- Retain the 2-second flush interval and process during system downtime\n\nI'll focus on a targeted approach for the 8 brain mechanisms, strategically selecting which to prioritize for effective memory integration with the evolution system.\n\nThe architecture requires careful mechanism selection to optimize learning and adaptation while minimizing computational overhead.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"# Neuro-Memory + Evolution Integration Architecture\n\n## Recommended Architecture: Hybrid Event-Mesh + Direct API\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    EVOLUTION SYSTEM                         │\n│  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────────┐          │\n│  │ Proposal │───>│ Memory   │───>│ Template     │          │\n│  │ Trigger  │    │ Query    │    │ Generation   │          │\n│  └──────────┘    │ (sync)   │    └──────────────┘          │\n│                  └────┬─────┘                               │\n└───────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┘\n                        │\n                        ▼\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                  NEURO-MEMORY AGENT                         │\n│  ┌──────────────┐    ┌──────────────┐    ┌──────────────┐  │\n│  │ Fast Cache   │    │ Memory Store │    │ Surprise     │  │\n│  │ (Redis/TS)   │<──>│ (SQLite)     │<──>│ Engine       │  │\n│  └──────┬───────┘    └──────────────┘    └──────────────┘  │\n│         │                                                   │\n└─────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n          │\n          ▼\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                     EVENT MESH                              │\n│  Topics: evolution.events, memory.consolidate,              │\n│          memory.surprise.batch                              │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## 1. Integration Points\n\n### **A. Pre-Proposal Query (Synchronous, Critical Path)**\n**When:** Before template generation  \n**Mechanism:** Two-Stage Retrieval + Interference Detection\n\n```typescript\n// In evolution proposal generation\nasync function generateProposal(context: ProposalContext) {\n  // Stage 1: Quick pattern match (cache)\n  const similarFailures = await memory.query({\n    type: 'failure',\n    context: context.signature,\n    limit: 5,\n    useCache: true\n  });\n  \n  // Stage 2: Full retrieval if needed\n  if (similarFailures.some(f => f.confidence > 0.8)) {\n    const detailed = await memory.retrieve({\n      embeddings: context.embedding,\n      mechanisms: ['interference', 'forgetting'],\n      min Surprise: 0.3\n    });\n    \n    // Inject constraints into template\n    context.avoidPatterns = detailed.interferencePatterns;\n  }\n  \n  return templateEngine.generate(context);\n}\n```\n\n### **B. Post-Outcome Learning (Async via Event Mesh)**\n**When:** After proposal succeeds/fails  \n**Mechanism:** Batch Queue → Consolidation\n\n```typescript\n// Fire-and-forget to event mesh\nevolution.events.emit('proposal.outcome', {\n  proposalId,\n  type: 'code-change',\n  outcome: 'success' | 'failed',\n  metrics: { testsPassed: 5, coverageDelta: +2.3 },\n  context: { filesChanged, riskLevel },\n  timestamp: Date.now()\n});\n```\n\n### **C. Periodic Consolidation (Background)**\n**When:** Every 5 minutes + idle time  \n**Mechanism:** Consolidation + Bayesian Surprise + Forgetting\n\n---\n\n## 2. What to Store\n\n### **Core Memory Records**\n```typescript\ninterface EvolutionMemory {\n  // Identity\n  id: string;\n  proposalType: string;        // 'code-change', 'refactor', 'bugfix'\n  signature: string;           // Hash of context features\n  \n  // Learning signals\n  outcome: 'success' | 'failed' | 'partial';\n  surpriseScore?: number;      // Computed async\n  confidence: number;          // Model's confidence at proposal time\n  \n  // Context (for retrieval)\n  contextEmbedding: number[];  // 384-dim embedding\n  features: {\n    filesChanged: string[];\n    riskLevel: number;\n    testCoverage: number;\n    complexity: number;\n  };\n  \n  // Temporal\n  timestamp: number;\n  accessCount: number;\n  lastAccessed: number;\n  \n  // Outcome details\n  errorSignature?: string;     // Stack trace hash\n  failureReason?: string;\n  successMetrics?: object;\n}\n```\n\n### **Surprise Score (Compute Async)**\n```python\n# Batch compute during consolidation\nasync def compute_surprise_batch(events: List[Event]):\n    # Use lightweight KL-divergence approximation\n    for event in events:\n        expected = predict_outcome(event.context)\n        actual = event.outcome\n        event.surpriseScore = kl_divergence(expected, actual)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Mechanism Usage by Integration Point\n\n| Mechanism | Pre-Proposal | Post-Outcome | Consolidation |\n|-----------|-------------|--------------|---------------|\n| **Two-Stage Retrieval** | ✅ Primary | ❌ | ❌ |\n| **Interference Detection** | ✅ Avoid conflicts | ❌ | ✅ Prune |\n| **Bayesian Surprise** | ❌ Too slow | ❌ | ✅ Batch compute |\n| **Consolidation** | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ Primary |\n| **Forgetting** | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ Decay weak memories |\n| **Pattern Recognition** | ✅ Quick match | ❌ | ✅ Extract patterns |\n| **Embedding Search** | ✅ Similarity | ❌ | ✅ Index |\n| **Error Clustering** | ❌ | ✅ Cluster failures | ✅ Refine |\n\n### **Mechanism Details:**\n\n**Two-Stage Retrieval (Pre-Proposal)**\n- Stage 1: Bloom filter in cache → quick rejection of irrelevant patterns\n- Stage 2: Full embedding search only if Stage 1 matches\n- Latency: <50ms for Stage 1, <200ms for Stage 2\n\n**Bayesian Surprise (Consolidation Only)**\n- Batch compute every 5 minutes\n- Use approximated KL-divergence (not full Bayesian)\n- Only for outcomes that contradict expectations\n- Threshold: surprise > 0.5 → boost memory strength\n\n**Forgetting (Consolidation)**\n```python\ndef apply_forgetting():\n    # Ebbinghaus forgetting curve\n    for memory in all_memories:\n        age_hours = (now - memory.timestamp) / 3600\n        decay = math.exp(-memory.accessCount / (1 + age_hours * 0.1))\n        \n        if decay < 0.1 and memory.surpriseScore < 0.3:\n            # Low-value, rarely accessed → delete\n            delete(memory)\n        elif decay < 0.3:\n            # Weaken but keep\n            memory.strength *= decay\n```\n\n**Interference Detection (Pre-Proposal + Consolidation)**\n- Pre-proposal: Check if new change conflicts with past failures\n- Consolidation: Identify contradictory memories and resolve\n\n---\n\n## 4. Performance Optimization Strategy\n\n### **Three-Tier Architecture**\n```\n┌─────────────────┐\n│  L1: In-Memory  │  10ms - Hot patterns (100 items)\n│  Bloom + Cache  │\n└────────┬────────┘\n         │\n┌────────▼────────┐\n│  L2: SQLite     │  50ms - Recent memories (10K items)\n│  + Embeddings   │\n└────────┬────────┘\n         │\n┌────────▼────────┐\n│  L3: Python     │  500ms - Full neuro mechanisms\n│  Neuro-Memory   │  (only during consolidation)\n└─────────────────┘\n```\n\n### **IPC Optimization**\n```typescript\n// Use Unix domain sockets, not HTTP\nconst NEURO_SOCKET = '/tmp/openclaw-neuro.sock';\n\n// Batch all Python calls during consolidation\nawait neuroClient.batch([\n  { method: 'computeSurprise', events: batch },\n  { method: 'consolidate', memories: recent },\n  { method: 'prune', threshold: 0.1 }\n]);\n```\n\n### **When to Use Python (Expensive)**\n- ❌ Pre-proposal query (use cache/SQLite instead)\n- ❌ Post-outcome logging (just queue it)\n- ✅ Consolidation (every 5 min)\n- ✅ Surprise batch computation (idle time)\n- ✅ Pattern extraction from failures (weekly)\n\n---\n\n## 5. Consolidation Strategy\n\n### **Schedule**\n```typescript\n// Every 5 minutes\ncron.schedule('*/5 * * * *', async () => {\n  await consolidation.run({\n    mechanisms: ['forgetting', 'interference'],\n    batchSize: 100\n  });\n});\n\n// Every 30 minutes (more expensive)\ncron.schedule('*/30 * * * *', async () => {\n  await consolidation.run({\n    mechanisms: ['surprise', 'pattern-extraction'],\n    batchSize: 50\n  });\n});\n\n// During idle (CPU < 20%)\nidleMonitor.onIdle(async () => {\n  await consolidation.run({\n    mechanisms: ['full-consolidation'],\n    throttle: true\n  });\n});\n```\n\n### **Consolidation Workflow**\n```python\nasync def consolidate():\n    # 1. Flush batch queue (already done by queue)\n    events = get_pending_events()\n    \n    # 2. Apply forgetting to existing memories\n    apply_forgetting_curve()\n    \n    # 3. Compute surprise for new outcomes\n    for event in events:\n        event.surprise = compute_surprise_approx(event)\n    \n    # 4. Detect interference patterns\n    conflicts = find_interference(events, existing_memories)\n    resolve_conflicts(conflicts)\n    \n    # 5. Cluster similar failures\n    clusters = cluster_failures(events)\n    for cluster in clusters:\n        create_pattern_memory(cluster)\n    \n    # 6. Update cache\n    update_l1_cache(get_hot_patterns())\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Retrieval Strategy\n\n### **Before Proposal Generation**\n```typescript\nasync function queryEvolutionMemory(context: ProposalContext) {\n  // Fast path: Check cache for exact signature\n  const cached = await cache.get(`sig:${context.signature}`);\n  if (cached) return cached;\n  \n  // Medium path: SQLite similarity search\n  const similar = await sqlite.query(`\n    SELECT * FROM memories \n    WHERE proposal_type = ? \n    AND outcome = 'failed'\n    ORDER BY vec_distance(embedding, ?) \n    LIMIT 5\n  `, [context.type, context.embedding]);\n  \n  // If high-confidence failure found, warn\n  if (similar.some(s => s.confidence > 0.8)) {\n    return {\n      warning: 'Similar proposal failed before',\n      failures: similar,\n      avoidPatterns: extractPatterns(similar)\n    };\n  }\n  \n  // Slow path: Full Python query (rare, only for high-risk)\n  if (context.riskLevel > 0.8) {\n    return await neuroClient.query({\n      embedding: context.embedding,\n      mechanisms: ['interference', 'pattern-recognition']\n    });\n  }\n  \n  return null;\n}\n```\n\n### **After Failure (Learning)**\n```typescript\n// Immediately: Log to batch queue\nmemoryQueue.add({\n  type: 'failure',\n  proposalId,\n  errorSignature: hashStackTrace(error),\n  context: proposalContext,\n  outcome: 'failed'\n});\n\n// Queue flushes every 2s → event mesh\n// Consolidation picks up every 5 min → Python neuro\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Recommended Implementation Order\n\n### **Phase 1: Foundation (Week 1)**\n1. Event mesh topics: `evolution.events`, `memory.consolidate`\n2. SQLite schema with embeddings\n3. Basic forget/decay on access count\n4. Batch queue integration\n\n### **Phase 2: Retrieval (Week 2)**\n1. Two-stage retrieval (cache + SQLite)\n2. Pre-proposal memory query\n3. Inject constraints into templates\n4. L1 cache for hot patterns\n\n### **Phase 3: Intelligence (Week 3-4)**\n1. Bayesian surprise (batch computation)\n2. Interference detection\n3. Pattern extraction from failures\n4. Consolidation scheduler\n\n### **Phase 4: Optimization (Week 5)**\n1. Unix socket IPC\n2. Idle-time consolidation\n3. Surprise score boosting\n4. Metrics and tuning\n\n---\n\n## 8. Key Design Decisions\n\n| Decision | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|-----------|\n| **Event-mesh vs Direct** | Hybrid | Mesh for learning, direct API for query |\n| **Surprise computation** | Batch only | Too expensive for critical path |\n| **Primary retrieval** | Two-stage | Fast cache, slow fallback |\n| **Consolidation** | Background + idle | Don't block evolution |\n| **Python usage** | Minimal | Only batch ops, never sync queries |\n| **Forgetting** | Time + access | Keep surprising failures longer |\n| **Storage** | SQLite + cache | Balance speed vs capability |\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n**Best Architecture:**\n- **Event-mesh for learning** (async, decoupled)\n- **Direct API for retrieval** (sync, fast)\n- **Three-tier cache** (memory → SQLite → Python)\n- **Batch consolidation** (every 5 min + idle)\n\n**When to use mechanisms:**\n- **Pre-proposal:** Two-stage retrieval, interference check (cache/SQLite)\n- **Post-outcome:** Queue event (no computation)\n- **Consolidation:** Surprise, forgetting, pattern extraction (Python batch)\n\n**Performance guardrails:**\n- Never call Python on critical path\n- L1 cache for 100 hot patterns (<10ms)\n- Surprise computed in batches only\n- 5-min consolidation window\n\nThis architecture keeps evolution responsive while enabling intelligent learning from failures."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"zhipu","model":"glm-5","usage":{"input":3006,"output":3475,"cacheRead":11200,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":17681,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1773007479664}}
